/-
# Locally finite groups with a full Sylow basis (Kourovka Notebook Problem 3.12)

Kourovka Notebook, Problem 3.12 (Yu. M. Gorchakov), marked solved in the 21st issue: (well-known
problem) is a locally finite group with a full Sylow basis locally soluble?
-/

module

public import Batteries.Util.ProofWanted
public import Mathlib.GroupTheory.Finiteness
public import Mathlib.GroupTheory.Solvable
public import Mathlib.GroupTheory.Sylow
public import Mathlib.Order.CompletePartialOrder

@[expose] public section

namespace MathlibExt.GroupTheory.FullSylowBasisLocallySolubleKourovka3Problem12Wanted

/-!
Source: E. I. Khukhro and V. D. Mazurov (eds.), Unsolved Problems in Group Theory: The Kourovka
Notebook, 21st issue, arXiv:1401.0300v49 (2026), Problem 3.12 (Yu. M. Gorchakov). UnsolvedMath
record UM-00700166 (`KOU-3.12`), dataset ulamai/UnsolvedMath v1.8.0.

The Notebook does not define a full Sylow basis; the accepted solution uses Golberg's definition
(Mat. Sb. 36 (1955), Definition 1, as reproduced by Starostin and Eidinov): Sylow `p`-subgroups
`P_p` (maximal `p`-subgroups, Mathlib's `Sylow`), one for each prime, such that the subgroup
generated by the `P_p` with `p ∈ J` is a `J`-group for every finite set `J` of primes, and fullness
means that all `P_p` generate `G`. The stronger convention that also asks `P_p P_q = P_q P_p` is not
covered. Locally finite and locally soluble are stated through finitely generated subgroups.
-/

/-- `P` is a full Sylow basis of `G` in Golberg's sense: `P p` is a Sylow `p`-subgroup (a maximal
`p`-subgroup) for each prime `p`, for every finite set `J` of primes the subgroup generated by the
`P p` with `p ∈ J` is a `J`-group (the orders of its elements have all prime divisors in `J`), and
the `P p` generate `G`. -/
def IsFullSylowBasis {G : Type} [Group G] (P : (p : Nat.Primes) → Sylow p G) : Prop :=
  (∀ J : Finset Nat.Primes, ∀ g ∈ ⨆ (p : Nat.Primes) (_ : p ∈ J), (P p).toSubgroup,
    IsOfFinOrder g ∧ ∀ q : ℕ, q.Prime → q ∣ orderOf g → ∃ p ∈ J, (p : ℕ) = q) ∧
  ⨆ p : Nat.Primes, (P p).toSubgroup = ⊤

/-- Kourovka 3.12 (Yu. M. Gorchakov): a locally finite group with a full Sylow basis is locally
soluble (every finitely generated subgroup is soluble). -/
def fullSylowBasisLocallySoluble : Prop :=
  ∀ (G : Type) [Group G], (∀ H : Subgroup G, H.FG → Finite H) →
    (∃ P : (p : Nat.Primes) → Sylow p G, IsFullSylowBasis P) →
    ∀ H : Subgroup G, H.FG → Group.IsSolvable H

/--
Resolved false: Jayadevan (Theorem 1.1) constructs a countable residually finite, locally finite
group containing `Sym(5)` (hence not locally soluble) that is generated by Sylow subgroups `P₂, P₃,
P₅` with `⟨P_p, P_q⟩` a `{p, q}`-group for `p ≠ q`, a full Sylow basis in Golberg's sense. The 21st
issue of the Kourovka Notebook marks 3.12 solved ("No, not always"; AI solution confirmed by A. A.
Buturlakin). Source: E. I. Khukhro and V. D. Mazurov (eds.), Unsolved Problems in Group Theory: The
Kourovka Notebook, 21st issue, arXiv:1401.0300v49 (2026), Problem 3.12 (Yu. M. Gorchakov);
resolution: Achyuth Jayadevan, A nonsoluble residually finite, locally finite group with a full
prime-support Sylow basis, preprint, Zenodo (12 September 2026), Theorem 1.1 with the definition
(1.1) of Section 1: a countable residually finite, locally finite group G with a full Sylow basis
(P_p) for p ∈ {2, 3, 5} and an embedding Sym(5) ↪ G, so G is not locally soluble; an AI solution
confirmed by A. A. Buturlakin, Kourovka Notebook (21st issue), Problem 3.12: No, not always,
https://zenodo.org/records/22721349.
-/
public theorem_wanted fullSylowBasisLocallySoluble_refuted : ¬ fullSylowBasisLocallySoluble

end MathlibExt.GroupTheory.FullSylowBasisLocallySolubleKourovka3Problem12Wanted
